    36	/**
    37	 * The claim door: `POST /v1/claims`, where the simulator asks for work.
    38	 *
    39	 * This group is deliberately unlike every other on the API, in three ways
    40	 * that are each contract rather than convenience.
    41	 *
    42	 * **The service token is the whole gate, and it resolves to nothing.** These
    43	 * routes never accept a customer key or a session — the deployment's own
    44	 * `EGMA_SIMULATOR_SERVICE_TOKEN`, compared in constant time, opens the door
    45	 * and becomes no context at all. Every claimed simulation instead arrives
    46	 * from the module with a context narrowed to that row's own organization and
    47	 * project, so the credential a simulator holds cannot widen into anybody's
    48	 * data even in principle: there is no context to widen.
    49	 *
    50	 * **The group sits outside the per-organization rate limit.** The budget
    51	 * exists so one customer's runaway loop is not everybody's problem, and it
    52	 * is keyed on the organization a credential resolves to; the simulator is
    53	 * egma's own service standing behind every organization at once, so a busy
    54	 * run must never eat any customer's budget from the inside — and there is no
    55	 * organization to key its own on. The token is the gate here, not a budget.
    56	 *
    57	 * **The claim is held open rather than answered empty.** Dispatch is pull,
    58	 * and the promise a developer actually feels is "a queued simulation is
    59	 * claimed within about a second". So an empty queue holds the request and
    60	 * re-asks every second until work arrives or the hold runs out — the long
    61	 * poll every pull-dispatch product ships. The client says how long it is
    62	 * willing to hang (`wait_seconds`), the server holds at most `min` of that
    63	 * and its own cap, and a client that said nothing gets a middling default —
    64	 * so a short-waiting client can never see its own request time out. A
    65	 * notification may one day replace the re-ask behind this same route;
    66	 * nothing the simulator sees would change.
